# Concept 1 - Launch Templates 
    A Launch Template is like a saved blueprint for launching EC2 instances.
    It stores things like:
    • AMI
    • Instance type
    • Security groups
    • Key pair
    • User data (startup script)
    • Storage configuration
    • Network settings

    Why it exists:
        So you don’t have to manually configure EC2 settings every time.
        You save the configuration once, then reuse it.
    A real‑world analogy:
        It’s like saving a “preset” for launching servers.
        Launch Templates are used heavily in:
        • Auto Scaling Groups
        • Spot Fleets
        • Reproducible deployments
        • Large‑scale architectures


# Concept 2 — Spot Requests 

    - asking AWS for discounted spare capacity
    - You tell AWS:
            • what instance type you want
            • how many
            • in which AZ
            • at what maximum price (optional)
    - AWS then tries to fulfill your request only if spare capacity exists.
    - If capacity disappears:
            • your instance is interrupted
            • you get a 2‑minute warning
            • AWS stops or terminates it (depending on your setting)
    - Why Spot Requests exist:
            To let you run:
            • batch jobs
            • data processing
            • CI/CD runners
            • stateless workloads
            • fault‑tolerant systems
            at a fraction of the cost.


